[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject))
and marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Read by tenant and body, its reports are more than one
problem. The queued parser fix closes only one of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Each problem below has its own cause and needs its own ticket and fix.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

Counts are unique reports: the export lists some reports twice, and each id is counted once.

## The problems

### `parser`: dates read month first

The shared parser reads every date month first. Our tenants all write the day first, so a booking for
7 April comes out as 4 July. It reaches every tenant, through the app, by email and through both
booking forms. This is the bug #4471 was opened for, and the fix that is queued.

`causes.ts` files booking-v3 form reports as a separate cause, `hint`: the form prints `MM/DD/YYYY`
under its date field. That is not a second problem. People who type month first, as the hint says, are
read month first by the parser, so the hint alone cannot flip a date. Those reports show the same flip as
every other `parser` report, and the same fix covers them.

### `shift`: the booking lands one day off

**Observed.** These reports are not a flip. The date moves by one day ("Tuesday 6 October showed as
Monday"), bookings in one month come out a day off, the same meeting falls on different days in Lisbon
and Toronto, and an invite arrives before its booking. A day/month swap cannot do that: it keeps the date
or moves it to another month. The desk had already read these as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`), but
counted them under `parser` by tenant.

**Not confirmed.** A time-zone or daylight-saving conversion of the invite would fit: different days in
two cities, and a shift that only shows in some months. Nobody has checked the code yet. Look at how
invite dates are converted between the tenant's and the recipient's time zone before calling it that.

## How to read a report

- Place a report by its tenant's locale and by what its body says, not by its subject.
  The same subjects come in for every problem above.
- Do not use the intake tag. It follows the reporter's browser, not the cause.

